new file mode 100644
index 0000000..360816e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,208 @@
+# Helpdesk API
+
+Rails API-only backend backed by PostgreSQL. The frontend is a separate application.
+
+## Run locally
+
+Use Ruby 4.0.7 (see `.ruby-version`) and set `DATABASE_URL` to your PostgreSQL connection URL.
+
+```sh
+bundle install
+bin/rails db:prepare
+bin/rails server
+```
+
+| Environment variable | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `DATABASE_URL` | PostgreSQL connection URL. |
+| `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL` | Frontend URL, default `http://localhost:3001`. Its origin is allowed by CORS and used for the email links. The frontend should serve `/verify-email` and `/reset-password` at that origin. |
+| `HELPDESK_MAIL_FROM` | From address, default `support@example.test`. Set this to an address authorized by your mail provider in production. |
+| `SMTP_ADDRESS` | Outbound SMTP server. Without it, development writes messages to `tmp/mail/`; production uses Action Mailer's default SMTP settings. |
+| `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_DOMAIN`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_PASSWORD`, `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` | Optional SMTP settings when `SMTP_ADDRESS` is set. The port defaults to `587` and authentication to `plain` when a username is supplied. |
+
+In production, also set `SECRET_KEY_BASE`. Use HTTPS for the frontend and API.
+
+## JSON API
+
+All paths in the table are relative to `/api/v1`. Send `Content-Type: application/json` for JSON bodies. Except for `POST /account/create`, send `X-Helpdesk-Account` with the business's subdomain. Authenticated endpoints also require `Authorization: Bearer <session.token>`.
+
+| Method and path | Body | Successful response |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /account/create` | `{"account":{"name":"Acme","subdomain":"acme","admin":{"name":"Jo","email":"jo@acme.test","password":"my-first-password"}}}` | `201` with `account` and an unverified admin `user`; sends a verification email. |
+| `GET /portal` | — | `200` with `portal.name` and `portal.subdomain`. Public. |
+| `POST /registrations` | `{"user":{"name":"Avery","email":"avery@example.test","password":"my-customer-password"}}` | `201` with an unverified `user` for the selected business; sends a verification email. Public. |
+| `GET /account` | — | `200` with `account`. Authenticated. |
+| `POST /sessions` | `{"session":{"email":"jo@acme.test","password":"my-first-password"}}` | `201` with `account`, `user`, and `session.token`. The email must be verified. |
+| `DELETE /session` | — | `204`; revokes the supplied session. |
+| `GET /me` | — | `200` with the authenticated `user`. |
+| `PATCH /me` | `{"user":{"name":"Jo Smith"}}` | `200` with the updated `user`. |
+| `PUT /me/password` | `{"password_change":{"current_password":"my-first-password","password":"my-new-password"}}` | `204`; revokes all of the user's sessions and pending password-reset links. |
+| `POST /email_verifications` | `{"email_verification":{"email":"jo@acme.test"}}` | `202`; sends a replacement link if the email needs verification. |
+| `POST /email_verifications/confirm` | `{"email_verification":{"token":"TOKEN"}}` | `200` with the verified `user`. |
+| `POST /password_resets` | `{"password_reset":{"email":"jo@acme.test"}}` | `202`; sends a replacement link if the email can be recovered in this business. |
+| `POST /password_resets/confirm` | `{"password_reset":{"token":"TOKEN","password":"my-new-password"}}` | `204`; consumes the link and revokes all of the user's sessions. |
+| `POST /account/cancel` | — | `200` with the cancelled `account`. Owner only. |
+| `POST /account/reactivate` | — | `200` with the reactivated `account`. Owner only. |
+
+Email addresses and subdomains are case-insensitive. An email is unique within its business, not across businesses. Verification and reset links are single-use and expire after 24 hours and one hour, respectively. A new link of the same kind replaces the previous one. The frontend gets the `account` and `token` from the URL and uses them as the tenant header and JSON token on confirmation. Passwords must be non-blank and at most 72 bytes (the bcrypt limit). Sessions expire after 30 days and can also be explicitly revoked.
+
+## Portal conversations
+
+Customers register as `member` users and use the same email verification, sign-in, profile, and password recovery endpoints as staff. Customers can only access their own requests. Staff (`admin` and `agent` users) can access all tickets in their business. Every conversation route requires a verified session for an active business.
+
+| Method and path | Body | Successful response |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /requests` | `{"request":{"subject":"Cannot download my receipt","comment":{"body":"The download button does nothing."}}}` | `201` with the new, open `request`. |
+| `GET /requests` / `GET /tickets` | — | `200` with `requests` or `tickets` and pagination metadata. |
+| `GET /requests/:id` / `GET /tickets/:id` | — | `200` with a `request` or `ticket`. |
+| `GET /requests/:id/comments` / `GET /tickets/:id/comments` | — | `200` with `comments` and pagination metadata. |
+| `PATCH /requests/:id` | `{"request":{"comment":{"body":"It works now."},"status":"solved"}}` | `200` with the updated `request`. |
+| `PATCH /tickets/:id` | `{"ticket":{"comment":{"body":"Please try again now."},"status":"pending"}}` | `200` with the updated `ticket`. |
+| `POST /tickets/:id/merge` | `{"merge":{"target_id":57}}` | `200` with the updated `source` and `target` tickets (staff only). |
+
+Requests and tickets expose `id`, `subject`, `status`, `requester_id`, `created_at`, and `updated_at`. Tickets also include `requester` with `id`, `name`, and `email`. Comments include `id`, `body`, `author` (`id` and `name`), `public`, and `created_at`.
+
+A PATCH can include a reply, a status change, or both. Customers can mark a request `solved`; replying reopens a `pending` or `solved` request unless the same update marks it solved. Staff can set `open`, `pending`, or `solved`, and a staff reply without a status leaves the status unchanged. Replies are public by default. Staff can send `"public":false` inside `comment` to add a note visible only to staff. Replies do not send email.
+
+Staff can merge two distinct, non-closed tickets for the same customer. The source stays readable with its original comments and becomes `closed`. Both ticket and request responses include `merged_into_id` (`null` if unmerged), and both lists accept `?status=closed`. Closed tickets reject updates and inbound email replies. If the target is later merged, the original link still points to that target.
+
+Each side gets a private merge note by default. To override either note, pass `source_comment` or `target_comment` inside `merge`, with a `body` string and an optional boolean `public`. Merge notes never send email, including public notes.
+
+Ticket, request, and comment lists still accept positive integer `page` and `per_page` query parameters (defaults `1` and `25`). These responses include `count` (all visible records before pagination), `page`, `per_page`, `next_page`, and `previous_page`; `next_page` is `null` when there are no more records, and `previous_page` is `null` on page 1. In this mode, requests and tickets are ordered by most recent activity, and comments are ordered oldest first.
+
+### Cursor pagination
+
+All collections support cursors: tickets, requests, both comment lists, users,
+staff, invitations, groups, and group memberships. Opt in with a nested `page`
+query parameter, for example `GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open`.
+Without a nested `page`, each collection keeps its existing response format.
+
+Cursor responses retain the collection key and add `meta` and `links`. An empty
+page looks like this:
+
+```json
+{"tickets":[],"meta":{"has_more":false,"after_cursor":null,"before_cursor":null},"links":{"next":null,"prev":null}}
+```
+
+For a nonempty page, `before_cursor` marks the first record and `after_cursor`
+marks the last. Follow `links.next` or `links.prev` with the usual account and
+authorization headers; the relative URLs retain the filters and page size. Or
+send `page[after]` or `page[before]` with the corresponding cursor, keeping the
+same filters. `page[size]` accepts 1–100 and defaults to 25. `has_more` refers
+to the direction of the request: forward by default or with `page[after]`,
+backward with `page[before]`. A link is `null` if there are no results in that
+direction. Cursor pagination does not return a total count.
+
+Cursors are opaque and tied to the endpoint, account, signed-in user, and
+filters. Invalid cursors, supplying both directions, or combining a nested
+`page` with `per_page` returns `400`.
+
+Cursor mode orders tickets and requests newest created first, comments oldest
+created first, and other collections by ascending ID. Creation-time ties use
+ID as the tie-breaker. Edits and replies do not move records in this order, and
+a cursor still works if its boundary record is deleted. Pages are live, not a
+snapshot: newly added records and changes to filter matches can affect what is
+visible on later requests.
+
+## Cancellation and purge
+
+Cancelling immediately revokes every session for the business and blocks portal and ordinary account access. For 90 days, the verified owner can sign in again (or reset their password if needed) and use the new bearer token to reactivate. The same token can be used for ordinary requests once reactivation succeeds.
+
+Schedule this command periodically (for example, daily) in the deployed environment:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails helpdesk:purge_cancelled_accounts
+```
+
+It permanently deletes accounts that have been cancelled for at least 90 days, along with their users, groups, memberships, sessions, email links, tickets, and comments. Active and more recently cancelled accounts are left intact.
+
+## Tests
+
+Point `DATABASE_URL` at a **dedicated test database**, not a development or production database:
+
+```sh
+export DATABASE_URL=postgresql://localhost/helpdesk_test
+RAILS_ENV=test bin/rails db:prepare
+bin/rails test
+```
+
+### Staff and invitations
+
+Within `/api/v1`, admins can create, list, inspect, resend and revoke
+`invitations`. Lists accept `status=pending|accepted|revoked|expired`.
+Invitations last seven days; resending a pending invitation rotates the link
+and renews its expiry. Preview and acceptance require the account header and
+link token, but no bearer session.
+
+Admins can list and inspect `users`, filter with `role` and/or
+`suspended=true|false`, and patch `user.suspended`. Admins and agents can
+list active `staff` and update `ticket.assignee_id` (use `null` to clear it).
+Assignment changes and private comments (`ticket.comment.public: false`)
+do not affect customer request timestamps or expose staff-only data.
+
+Staff ticket responses include `priority` (`normal` by default or `high`). Set it
+with `PATCH /tickets/:id` and `{"ticket":{"priority":"high"}}`.
+Staff can combine `status`, `assignee_id`, `requester_id`, `priority`, and `q`
+on `GET /tickets`. Use numeric IDs for people or `assignee_id=unassigned` for
+unassigned tickets. `q` matches a literal, case-insensitive substring in the
+subject or any public comment. Filters apply before pagination.
+Customers can use `status` on `GET /requests` to filter their own requests;
+priority is not exposed or writable on customer endpoints.
+
+### Groups and ticket routing
+
+Admins manage groups and memberships. Admins and agents can browse both resources
+in ascending ID order. Group names are unique within the account, ignoring case.
+Groups organize the inbox; staff can still access all
+tickets in their account, whether or not they belong to the ticket's group.
+
+| Method and path | Body | Successful response |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `GET /groups` | — | `200` with `groups`. |
+| `GET /groups/:id` | — | `200` with `group`. |
+| `POST /groups` | `{"group":{"name":"Billing"}}` | `201` with `group`. |
+| `PATCH /groups/:id` | `{"group":{"name":"Accounts"}}` | `200` with `group`. |
+| `DELETE /groups/:id` | — | `204`. |
+| `GET /group_memberships` | — | `200` with `group_memberships`. Accepts `group_id` and `user_id` filters. |
+| `GET /group_memberships/:id` | — | `200` with `group_membership`. |
+| `POST /group_memberships` | `{"group_membership":{"group_id":20,"user_id":12,"default":true}}` | `201` with `group_membership`. |
+| `PUT /group_memberships/:id/make_default` | — | `200` with the new default `group_membership`. |
+| `DELETE /group_memberships/:id` | — | `204`. |
+
+The first membership becomes the staff member's default automatically. Creating
+another membership with `default: true` or calling `make_default` replaces the
+previous default. Deleting a default promotes the oldest remaining membership
+if there is one. Suspension clears assignments but preserves memberships and defaults.
+
+Staff tickets include `group_id` and `group` (`id` and `name`), both `null` when
+ungrouped. Filter the inbox with `GET /tickets?group_id=20` or
+`GET /tickets?group_id=unassigned`. To move a ticket, send `ticket.group_id` in
+`PATCH /tickets/:id`. The assignee must belong to the destination group; moving
+without specifying a new assignee clears the current assignee if they do not
+belong. Assigning an ungrouped ticket uses the assignee's default group, if any.
+Setting `assignee_id` to `null` leaves the group in place. Setting `group_id` to
+`null` clears both fields. Deleting a membership clears that staff member's
+assignments in that group; deleting a group ungroups its tickets and clears
+their assignees. Customer requests do not expose or accept group routing fields.
+
+### Email support
+
+Run `bin/rails db:migrate` with `DATABASE_URL` set. Set
+`HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN` (defaults to `support.example.test`) and configure the
+SMTP relay for that domain to forward RFC 822 messages to
+`/rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails` over HTTPS. Use content type
+`message/rfc822` and HTTP Basic credentials with username `actionmailbox` and
+password `RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD`. The relay should accept business
+addresses such as `acme@support.example.test` and the `reply+…` addresses used in
+outgoing replies. Preserve recipient headers and enforce sender authentication
+at the relay: the app matches the parsed From address, which is not itself
+proof of ownership.
+
+Configure SMTP for outbound mail as above. Email-created customers verify their
+address using the verification link and can set a portal password using the
+password reset flow. Replying by email does not verify a portal account.
+
+Action Mailbox routes asynchronously outside test. The default Rails async
+adapter runs jobs in the web process; for durable production processing,
+configure a persistent Active Job backend and run its workers. Make the raw
+email storage accessible to both ingress and workers (the default is local disk).
